A||||14||KRMFiero||Round Lug: What to do?||||1||
Z||000000||KRMFiero||12-02-2000||08:02 PM||||I was pulling off my tire's today and there was only 1 lug nut left, Put my 3/4" socket to it, turned the rachet and it was so easy i took the socket off and noticed the lug is completly rounded.... what do I do?!?!?<P><BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||12-02-2000||08:03 PM||||Your going to have to try to bang a smaller socket over it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||12-02-2000||08:27 PM||||If they aren't super tight, the socket thing works. Mine were and i had to weld a nut to it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||12-02-2000||09:40 PM||||i have seen sockets that look like a reversed easy out with tapered threads in the socket. They are made for removing factory keyed lug nuts when the owner has misplace the key. Some larger tire shops will have one.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||12-03-2000||01:34 AM||||I heard about those, and I went looking everywhere, but nobody had them. Even Pontiac said they wouldn't help me (well, they would, if I towed my car there and paid them). Bastards.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe4x4||12-03-2000||05:08 AM||||I had that happen to me on my truck.<P>I used 13/16 socket from my tools..<BR>it worked ..( broke the socket thow)<P>good luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||12-03-2000||07:28 AM||||This sounds crazy but here is a method that may work.<BR> Soak the rounded lug nut with a penetrating oil and (SLIGHTLY) loosen the other lug nuts.<BR>Drive the car slowly for a short distance, around the yard or in your drive. this will sometimes work. Be gentle to avoid damaging your wheels or lugs.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||bubbajoexx||12-05-2000||12:36 PM||||USE A 18 MILIMETER SOCKET AND A HAMMER.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bubbajoexx||12-05-2000||12:37 PM||||USE A 18 MILIMETER SOCKET AND A HAMMER.||||reg||1
Z||000009||prof bobo||12-05-2000||02:53 PM||||If you got enough access, Dremel tool w/carbide cutter disk, cut a slot in the nut,  split it with a chisel.  <P>Didja know Dremel now makes semi-flexible carbides that don't shatter every minute or so?<P>Buy cheap ratchet extension and weld it on to the nut?||||reg||1
Z||000010||WampusCat||12-05-2000||02:57 PM||||If you can get at it with a grinder, grind two opposite sides flat and take it off with an adj. wrench. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||JohnnyK||12-05-2000||03:37 PM||||Yasee, that doesn't work. You actually ever tried getting a rounded nut off one of these wheels?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (there is NO room whatsoever. Not enough for a grinder, and certainly not enough for an adjustable wrench.)||||reg||1
Z||000012||alpha||12-05-2000||03:44 PM||||I had this happen once and I ended up drilling out the stud and nut together. It was a real *****  and I did some damage to the wheel (14" Turbo). In retrospect I would try anything but that! If it were to happen to me today, I would try welding a good sized nut onto the lug or I would look for the reverse easy out which does exist.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fiero.dhs.org/fiero/sig.jpg">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu">douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||KRMFiero||12-05-2000||03:49 PM||||Thanks Alot! I used a smalled socket (craftsmen), hammered it on, cracked the socket, but since I used a craftsmen I got a free new socket  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>thanx<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<BR>Front - AudioBahn 4 x 10<BR>Headrest - AudioBahn 3 1/2<P> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000014||Cheever2||12-05-2000||07:20 PM||||Now, yer absolutely certain it's not one of those that you have ta use a key to get it off?  Just a thot.<BR>||||reg||1
